---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<style>
  /* ── Layout ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  #story-loading {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: #64748b;
    background: white;
    z-index: 100;
  }
  :global(.dark) #story-loading,
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    #story-loading {
      background: #0f172a;
      color: #94a3b8;
    }
  }

  #story {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .story-chapter {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 300vh;
  }

  .sticky-graphic {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100vh;
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #f8fafc;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .sticky-graphic { background: #0f172a; }
  }

  .sticky-graphic svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 90vh;
  }

  /* D3 axis text picks up current text color */
  .sticky-graphic text {
    fill: currentColor;
    font-family: inherit;
  }
  .sticky-graphic .domain,
  .sticky-graphic .tick line {
    stroke: currentColor;
  }

  /* ── Scroll steps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .scroll-steps {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 2rem;
    width: 32%;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .step {
    min-height: 80vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .step-card {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.93);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: #1e293b;
    border-left: 3px solid transparent;
    transition: border-color 0.2s;
    width: 100%;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .step-card {
      background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.93);
      color: #e2e8f0;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
    }
  }

  .step.is-active .step-card {
    border-left-color: #3b82f6;
  }

  .step-card h3 {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    color: #0f172a;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .step-card h3 { color: #f1f5f9; }
  }

  .step-card .stat {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #3b82f6;
    display: block;
    margin: 0.25rem 0;
  }

  /* ── Chapter titles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .chapter-title {
    position: absolute;
    top: 1.5rem;
    left: 2rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #0f172a;
    z-index: 10;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .chapter-title { color: #f1f5f9; }
  }

  /* ── Tooltips ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  #map-tooltip,
  #stations-tooltip {
    position: fixed;
    display: none;
    background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95);
    color: #f1f5f9;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 200;
    white-space: nowrap;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);
    border: 1px solid rgba(148,163,184,0.2);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  #map-tooltip { white-space: nowrap; }

  #stations-tooltip :global(strong) { display: block; font-size: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  #stations-tooltip :global(.tt-district) {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #94a3b8;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  #stations-tooltip :global(.tt-row) {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }
  #stations-tooltip :global(.tt-label) { color: #94a3b8; }
  #stations-tooltip :global(.tt-value) { font-weight: 600; color: #e2e8f0; }

  /* ── Intro chapter ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  #chapter-intro .sticky-graphic {
    background: #f0f6ff;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    #chapter-intro .sticky-graphic { background: #0c1629; }
  }

  /* ── Responsive ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .story-chapter { min-height: unset; }
    .sticky-graphic { position: relative; height: 60vw; min-height: 280px; }
    .scroll-steps {
      position: static;
      width: 100%;
      padding: 0 1rem 2rem;
    }
    .step { min-height: unset; padding: 0.5rem 0; }
    .chapter-title { font-size: 1rem; }
  }
</style>

<Layout>
  <div id="story-loading">Loading data…</div>
  <div id="map-tooltip"></div>
  <div id="stations-tooltip"></div>

  <main id="story" class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">

    <!-- ── Chapter 1: Introduction / Station Map ── -->
    <section class="story-chapter" id="chapter-intro">
      <div class="sticky-graphic">
        <span class="chapter-title">01 — Oslo's Bike Network</span>
        <svg id="viz-map"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-steps">

        <div class="step" data-chapter="intro" data-step="0">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Oslo Bysykkel</h3>
            <p>
              Oslo's city bike scheme launched in April 2019 with
              <strong>292 stations</strong> spread across the inner city.
              Each dot on the map represents one docking station.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.8rem; color:#64748b;">
              Background shows Oslo's sub-districts (delbydeler).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="intro" data-step="1">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Station popularity</h3>
            <p>
              Not all stations are equally busy. Circle size now reflects
              total trip volume — the largest circles mark the true hubs
              of Oslo's bike network.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
              Hover over any station to see its name and total trip count.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Chapter 2: Temporal / Yearly totals ── -->
    <section class="story-chapter" id="chapter-temporal">
      <div class="sticky-graphic">
        <span class="chapter-title">02 — Ridership Over the Years</span>
        <svg id="viz-yearly"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-steps">

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="0">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2019 — Launch year</h3>
            <span class="stat">~2.2M trips</span>
            <p>
              Oslo Bysykkel opened to great enthusiasm in April 2019.
              Over two million trips were recorded in the scheme's
              first season — a high-water mark that has not been matched since.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="1">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2020 — Pandemic year</h3>
            <span class="stat">~1.7M trips</span>
            <p>
              COVID-19 lockdowns emptied city streets and ridership fell sharply.
              The decline from 2019 was the steepest single-year drop in the network's history.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="2">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2021 — Continued decline</h3>
            <span class="stat">~1.4M trips</span>
            <p>
              Despite restrictions easing, ridership kept falling.
              Usage did not bounce back — it appears 2019 attracted
              a wave of curious first-time users who did not return.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="3">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2022 — Still sliding</h3>
            <span class="stat">~1.3M trips</span>
            <p>
              A further drop despite a return to normalcy in Oslo.
              The data suggest a structural shift, not a temporary disruption.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="4">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2023 — Levelling off</h3>
            <span class="stat">~1.1M trips</span>
            <p>
              Ridership stabilises around one million trips per year —
              roughly half the 2019 peak. This may represent the network's
              steady-state loyal user base.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="5">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2024 — Plateau</h3>
            <span class="stat">~1.1M trips</span>
            <p>
              For the second year running, totals hold near 1.1 million.
              The decline appears to have bottomed out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="temporal" data-step="6">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2025 — Partial year</h3>
            <p>
              Data through spring 2025 tracks the same seasonal pattern
              as recent years, with no sign of a recovery to 2019 levels.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Chapter 3: Seasonal patterns ── -->
    <section class="story-chapter" id="chapter-seasonal">
      <div class="sticky-graphic">
        <span class="chapter-title">03 — The Rhythm of the Seasons</span>
        <svg id="viz-seasonal"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-steps">

        <div class="step" data-chapter="seasonal" data-step="0">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>A summer sport</h3>
            <p>
              Oslo's bike season is strikingly short. In 2019, nearly all
              trips happened between <strong>May and October</strong>.
              The January–March period is almost silent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="seasonal" data-step="1">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2020 — Lower peaks</h3>
            <p>
              The pandemic year's summer peak dropped to ~298k trips in June —
              well below 2019's ~399k August peak.
              The seasonal shape remains, but everything shifts down.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="seasonal" data-step="2">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2021–2022 — Peaks keep falling</h3>
            <p>
              Even as lockdowns ended, summer peaks continued to shrink —
              217k in 2021, then 207k in 2022.
              The network never recovered its 2019 heights.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="seasonal" data-step="3">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>All years compared</h3>
            <p>
              The <strong>shape</strong> of the curve is consistent across all years —
              a sharp spring ramp, a broad summer plateau, a rapid autumn decline.
              But each successive year's curve sits lower than the last.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="seasonal" data-step="4">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>2023–2025 — Settling at a lower level</h3>
            <p>
              Recent years cluster near 175–192k trips at peak, roughly
              <strong>half</strong> the 2019 maximum.
              The seasonal rhythm is unchanged — the audience has shrunk.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Chapter 4: Top stations ── -->
    <section class="story-chapter" id="chapter-stations">
      <div class="sticky-graphic">
        <span class="chapter-title">04 — Where People Ride</span>
        <svg id="viz-stations"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-steps">

        <div class="step" data-chapter="stations" data-step="0">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Top 5 stations</h3>
            <p>
              The five busiest stations are all neighbourhood hubs —
              lively squares and parks that attract both commuters and leisure riders.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="stations" data-step="1">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Top 10 stations</h3>
            <p>
              The gap between rank 1 and rank 10 is already large.
              A power-law distribution is emerging — a handful of locations
              dominate total trip volume.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="stations" data-step="2">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Top 20 stations</h3>
            <p>
              Even at 20 stations, the long tail of the distribution is clear.
              The top 20 stations (out of 292) account for a disproportionate
              share of all trips made in the system.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Chapter 5: Trip duration ── -->
    <section class="story-chapter" id="chapter-duration">
      <div class="sticky-graphic">
        <span class="chapter-title">05 — How Long Do People Ride?</span>
        <svg id="viz-duration"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-steps">

        <div class="step" data-chapter="duration" data-step="0">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Average trip duration</h3>
            <p>
              Trip duration follows a clear seasonal rhythm.
              Summer trips average <strong>~14 minutes</strong>,
              while winter trips drop to around <strong>10 minutes</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="duration" data-step="1">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Volume vs. duration</h3>
            <p>
              Adding trip counts reveals that
              <span style="color:#f59e0b; font-weight:600">volume</span> and
              <span style="color:#3b82f6; font-weight:600">duration</span>
              move <em>together</em> — both peak in summer, both drop in winter.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
              Winter riders are the few committed commuters taking quick trips.
              Summer brings recreational riders who stay out longer.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step" data-chapter="duration" data-step="2">
          <div class="step-card">
            <h3>Summer months highlighted</h3>
            <p>
              The shaded bands mark June–August each year.
              Both trip volume and average duration are highest in this window —
              Oslo's summer cyclists ride more often <em>and</em> for longer.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</Layout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/story/story-main';
</script>
